;=======================================================================
; For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that
; whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting
; life.
;                                              - John 3:16, KJV
;=======================================================================
; EGT80 - "Ethernet Gateway Terminal"
;
; A CP/M terminal program for the Ethernet Gateway, written for real Z80
; hardware as much as the gateway's own CP/M emulator.  Unlike the period
; terminals it replaces, the serial port is chosen at RUN TIME from a menu
; instead of being fixed when the program is assembled - one EGT80.COM
; serves an RC2014, an Altair, an SC126, or the emulator.
;
; Ricky Bryce.  Part of the Ethernet Gateway project.
;
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ASSEMBLER COMPATIBILITY
;
; This source is written to assemble with PERIOD tooling - SLR Z80ASM
; (Z80ASM EGT80/A), Microsoft M80, and ZMAC - so it can be rebuilt on a
; real CP/M machine, not just cross-assembled.  That imposes rules;
; please keep to them when editing:
;
;   - No macros, no conditional assembly, no local/temporary labels.
;   - Upper-case Zilog mnemonics, one statement per line.
;   - Labels unique within their first SIX characters.
;   - Directives limited to EQU, ORG, DB, DW, DS, END.
;   - Expressions limited to + - * / AND OR SHL SHR and '$'.
;   - Plain 7-bit ASCII, no tabs inside quoted strings, <= 80 columns.
;   - CRLF line endings (the Makefile stages them; an LF-only file makes
;     M80 and ZMAC assemble almost nothing while reporting success).
;
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; COMPLETE: all five port families (Z80 SIO/2, 6850 ACIA, RomWBW HBIOS,
; Z180 ASCI, CP/M AUX:), the two-step port menu, settings saved into the
; program's own file, and XMODEM file transfer in both directions.
;
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; HOW THE HOT PATH IS KEPT CHEAP
;
; Two indirections, both resolved before the loop runs:
;
;   Port    - four RAM vectors (VPST/VPIN/VPOST/VPOUT) are rewritten when
;             the user picks a port, so selecting a port costs nothing per
;             byte.  This is the shape QTERM's overlays use, except ours
;             is chosen at run time rather than at assembly time.
;   Console - three RAM vectors (VCST/VCIN/VCOUT) are pointed either at
;             the BIOS console entries (CP/M 2.2, far cheaper per byte
;             than a BDOS call) or at BDOS-6 wrappers (CP/M 3), decided
;             once at startup.  The loop never tests the version.
;
; The terminal loop deliberately keeps NO state in registers across those
; calls: everything it needs is in A or in memory.  That removes any need
; for the vectors to preserve registers - which matters because on CP/M 3
; they land in the BDOS, which preserves nothing.
;
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; REGISTER CONVENTION - read this before adding a counted loop
;
; The XMODEM code counts 128 bytes in B while calling routines that need
; counters of their own, and three of them (PSEND, XGETB, XACCUM) used BC
; for that.  Each one silently destroyed the caller's count, and the
; symptom was not subtle: the send loop walked off the end of the buffer
; and shovelled a megabyte of RAM at the wire.
;
; So: PSEND and XGETB preserve BC; XACCUM preserves BC and HL (the block
; loops walk the buffer with HL while it needs HL for the accumulators);
; COUT/CST/CIN preserve BC, DE and HL.  The port and console vectors
; preserve NOTHING - call them only with everything you care about either
; in A or in memory.
;
; And the order matters as much as the pushes: a routine that restores a
; register BEFORE its last call to a port vector has not restored it at
; all, because the driver sets B to address the port.  PSEND had exactly
; that bug - it popped BC and then jumped to the output vector - and the
; symptom was the same runaway buffer walk as forgetting the push.
;=======================================================================

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CP/M system equates
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
BDOS    EQU     0005H           ; BDOS entry point
WBOOT   EQU     0000H           ; warm boot (exit to CCP)
BIOSPT  EQU     0001H           ; word: address of the BIOS WBOOT entry
BCONOU  EQU     2               ; console output (no reserved values)
BCONIO  EQU     6               ; direct console I/O (CP/M 2.2 and 3)
BVERS   EQU     12              ; return CP/M version
BOPEN   EQU     15              ; open file
BCLOSE  EQU     16              ; close file
BSETDMA EQU     26              ; set the DMA (record buffer) address
BWRRAN  EQU     34              ; write random record
BRDBUF  EQU     10              ; read console buffer (a line)
BDELET  EQU     19              ; delete file
BREADS  EQU     20              ; read sequential record
BWRITS  EQU     21              ; write sequential record
BMAKE   EQU     22              ; create file
BSEARF  EQU     17              ; search for first match

; Direct-console-I/O sub-codes used on CP/M 3
DCIRD   EQU     0FFH            ; read a character
DCIST   EQU     0FEH            ; console status

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Character equates
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
BEL     EQU     07H
BS      EQU     08H
TAB     EQU     09H
LF      EQU     0AH
CR      EQU     0DH
ESC     EQU     1BH
DEL     EQU     7FH

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XMODEM protocol bytes and bounds
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SOH     EQU     01H             ; start of a 128-byte block
EOT     EQU     04H             ; end of transmission
ACK     EQU     06H             ; block accepted
NAK     EQU     15H             ; block rejected / send in checksum mode
CAN     EQU     18H             ; cancel (two in a row)
CRCC    EQU     'C'             ; receiver asking for CRC mode
XRETRY  EQU     10              ; retries per block, and 'C' attempts
XNOISE  EQU     255             ; junk bytes tolerated before a transfer
XSHORT  EQU     1               ; inter-byte wait, in XGETB passes
XLONG   EQU     6               ; wait for a block / handshake

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Port kinds.  Only SIO is implemented in phase 1; the rest are listed
; here because the settings block records the kind numerically and the
; numbering must not shift when they arrive.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PKNONE  EQU     0               ; nothing selected
PKSIO   EQU     1               ; Z80 SIO/2
PKACIA  EQU     2               ; 6850 ACIA        (phase 2)
PKHBIO  EQU     3               ; RomWBW HBIOS     (phase 2)
PKASCI  EQU     4               ; Z180 ASCI        (phase 2)
PKAUX   EQU     5               ; CP/M BDOS AUX    (phase 2)

; Build identity lives in MVER, in the strings, and nowhere else - a
; second copy in an equate only invites the two to drift apart.

        ORG     0100H

;=======================================================================
; ENTRY
;
; The first instruction jumps over the settings block, which sits at a
; FIXED, 128-byte-aligned address so that saving it rewrites one record of
; the file containing settings and nothing else - no risk of writing a
; half-open FCB or a live variable back into the image.  Period software
; put its patch area in exactly this place for exactly this reason.
;=======================================================================
START:  JP      BEGIN

;=======================================================================
; SETTINGS  (record 1 of the file: address 0180H, offset 80H)
;
; Defaults are the values that suit the most machines: an RC2014/RomWBW
; second SIO/2 channel, and ANSI pass-through, which is what the gateway
; offers a terminal that asks for colour.  CFGCHK validates all of this at
; startup and falls back to these defaults if a saved block is damaged.
;=======================================================================
        ORG     0180H
CFGSIG: DB      'EGT80CFG'      ; must match CFGREF or the block is ignored
PKIND:  DB      PKSIO           ; selected port kind
PBASE:  DB      82H             ; SIO/2 ch B, ACIA base, or HBIOS unit
PCHAN:  DB      0               ; Z180 ASCI channel (0 or 1); unused elsewhere
PABASE: DB      0               ; Z180 internal I/O base for the ASCI family.
                                ; Its own byte, NOT shared with PBASE: PBASE
                                ; holds a port address for SIO/ACIA and a unit
                                ; number for HBIOS, and selecting ASCI does not
                                ; otherwise set it - so sharing meant ASCI
                                ; inherited whatever the last family used and
                                ; addressed registers that do not exist.
FLTMOD: DB      0               ; 0 = ANSI pass, 1 = ASCII strip
ECHOFL: DB      0               ; local echo off (the remote echoes)
CRLFFL: DB      0               ; do not add LF to a typed CR
ESCCH:  DB      19H             ; ^Y, as the QTERM builds here use

CFGADR  EQU     0180H           ; where the block lives
CFGREC  EQU     1               ; ...which is file record 1

        ORG     0200H

BEGIN:  LD      SP,STKTOP       ; our stack, not the CCP's
        CALL    CONSET          ; choose the console I/O path
        CALL    CFGCHK          ; trust the saved settings only if sane
        CALL    PORTST          ; point the port vectors at the default
        CALL    SCTTL           ; title screen
        JP      MAINLP          ; main menu

;=======================================================================
; CONSET - decide how console I/O is done, once.
;
; CP/M 2.2: take the BIOS console entries from page zero.  The word at
; 0001H points at the BIOS WBOOT entry, which is the SECOND slot of the
; jump table, so the console entries sit at fixed offsets from it:
; CONST = +3, CONIN = +6, CONOUT = +9.
;
; CP/M 3: the BIOS is banked and must not be called directly, so use
; BDOS 6 (direct console I/O), which exists on both systems.
;=======================================================================
CONSET: LD      C,BVERS
        CALL    BDOS
        LD      (CPMVER),A
        CP      30H             ; CP/M 3.0 or later?
        JR      NC,CON3
        LD      HL,(BIOSPT)     ; BIOS WBOOT entry
        INC     HL
        INC     HL
        INC     HL              ; CONST
        LD      (VCST+1),HL
        INC     HL
        INC     HL
        INC     HL              ; CONIN
        LD      (VCIN+1),HL
        INC     HL
        INC     HL
        INC     HL              ; CONOUT
        LD      (VCOUT+1),HL
        RET
CON3:   LD      HL,B6ST
        LD      (VCST+1),HL
        LD      HL,B6IN
        LD      (VCIN+1),HL
        LD      HL,B6OUT
        LD      (VCOUT+1),HL
        RET

;=======================================================================
; BDOS-6 console wrappers (the CP/M 3 path).
;
; They present the BIOS calling convention the rest of the program uses:
; status returns A=0 for "no key", input returns the byte in A, output
; takes the byte in C.
;=======================================================================
B6ST:   LD      C,BCONIO
        LD      E,DCIST
        CALL    BDOS
        OR      A
        RET
B6IN:   LD      C,BCONIO
        LD      E,DCIRD
        CALL    BDOS
        OR      A
        JR      Z,B6IN          ; no key yet: ask again
        RET
; B6OUT - console output on CP/M 3.
;
; Direct console I/O reserves three values of E: FFh, FEh and FDh mean
; "read", "status" and "read, waiting" - so passing a DATA byte of FDh or
; above through function 6 does not print it.  It performs an input
; instead, silently dropping the byte and, worse, eating a keystroke the
; user had typed.  A terminal passing 8-bit data through would hit this.
;
; Those three go out through function 2 instead, which has no reserved
; values.  Function 2 expands tabs, which is why it is not used for
; everything - but FDh..FFh are not tabs, so nothing is lost.
B6OUT:  LD      A,C
        CP      0FDH
        JR      NC,B6OUT1
        LD      E,A
        LD      C,BCONIO
        JP      BDOS
B6OUT1: LD      E,A
        LD      C,BCONOU
        JP      BDOS

;=======================================================================
; Console helpers.  COUT takes the byte in A (the rest of the program
; thinks in A; the BIOS wants C).
;
; These preserve BC, DE and HL because what they call does not: a CP/M
; BIOS is free to destroy any register, and the BDOS certainly does.
; Assuming otherwise cost an afternoon - printing a string through an
; unprotected wrapper emitted its first character forever, because the
; console call had left the string pointer pointing at itself.
;
; The cost is 66 T-states on a path that is already waiting on a UART at
; a millisecond per byte, which is the right trade: the terminal loop is
; made cheap by holding no state across calls at all, not by shaving
; pushes off the one routine every screen depends on.
;
; CST also normalises the flags, since neither a BIOS console-status
; routine nor the BDOS promises that Z reflects the value it returns.
;=======================================================================
CST:    PUSH    BC
        PUSH    DE
        PUSH    HL
        CALL    VCST
        POP     HL
        POP     DE
        POP     BC
        OR      A
        RET
CIN:    PUSH    BC
        PUSH    DE
        PUSH    HL
        CALL    VCIN
        POP     HL
        POP     DE
        POP     BC
        RET
COUT:   PUSH    AF
        PUSH    BC
        PUSH    DE
        PUSH    HL
        LD      C,A
        CALL    VCOUT
        POP     HL
        POP     DE
        POP     BC
        POP     AF
        RET

;=======================================================================
; CRLF - end a line.
;=======================================================================
CRLF:   LD      A,CR
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,LF
        JP      COUT

;=======================================================================
; PSTR - print the zero-terminated string at HL.
;
; Zero-terminated rather than '$'-terminated so that '$' can appear in
; text, and so printing does not depend on the BDOS being reachable.
;=======================================================================
PSTR:   LD      A,(HL)
        OR      A
        RET     Z
        CALL    COUT
        INC     HL
        JR      PSTR

;=======================================================================
; PHEX - print A as two hex digits.  Used for port addresses, which the
; user needs to see in the same form the hardware documentation uses.
;=======================================================================
PHEX:   PUSH    AF
        RRCA
        RRCA
        RRCA
        RRCA
        CALL    PHEXD
        POP     AF
        CALL    PHEXD
        RET
PHEXD:  AND     0FH
        ADD     A,90H
        DAA
        ADC     A,40H
        DAA
        JP      COUT

;=======================================================================
; UPCASE - fold the character in A to upper case, so menus accept either.
;=======================================================================
UPCASE: CP      'a'
        RET     C
        CP      'z'+1
        RET     NC
        SUB     20H
        RET


;=======================================================================
; PORT LAYER
;
; PORTST points the four port vectors at the driver for the selected kind.
; It runs at startup and whenever the user changes the port, so the
; vectors always agree with the settings block.  A driver is reached
; through those vectors, so the terminal loop pays one CALL per byte no
; matter how many families exist.
;
; PBASE means whatever the selected family needs: the status port for
; SIO/2 and the ACIA, the unit number for HBIOS, the internal I/O base
; for the Z180 ASCI (with the channel in PCHAN), and nothing at all for
; the CP/M AUX device.
;=======================================================================
PORTST: LD      A,(PKIND)
        CP      PKSIO
        JR      Z,PSSIO
        CP      PKACIA
        JR      Z,PSACI
        CP      PKHBIO
        JR      Z,PSHB
        CP      PKASCI
        JR      Z,PSASC
        CP      PKAUX
        JR      Z,PSAUX
        LD      HL,PNULL        ; nothing selected: safe stubs
        LD      DE,PNULL
        JR      PSSET4
PSSIO:  LD      HL,SIOST
        LD      DE,SIOIN
        CALL    PSSET2
        LD      HL,SIOOST
        LD      DE,SIOOUT
        JR      PSSETO
PSACI:  CALL    ACINIT          ; a 6850 needs programming before use
        LD      HL,ACIST
        LD      DE,ACIIN
        CALL    PSSET2
        LD      HL,ACIOST
        LD      DE,ACIOUT
        JR      PSSETO
PSHB:   LD      HL,HBST
        LD      DE,HBIN
        CALL    PSSET2
        LD      HL,HBOST
        LD      DE,HBOUT
        JR      PSSETO
PSASC:  CALL    ASCPAT          ; patch the register addresses in place
        LD      HL,ASCST
        LD      DE,ASCIN
        CALL    PSSET2
        LD      HL,ASCOST
        LD      DE,ASCOUT
        JR      PSSETO
PSAUX:  XOR     A
        LD      (AUXFLG),A      ; drop any byte held from a previous port
        LD      HL,AUXST
        LD      DE,AUXIN
        CALL    PSSET2
        LD      HL,AUXOST
        LD      DE,AUXOUT
        JR      PSSETO

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PSSET2 - point the input pair (status in HL, read in DE) at a driver;
; called twice, the second time for the output pair.  A tiny helper, but
; it turns five nearly identical driver-selection blocks into two lines
; each, which is the difference between adding a family being cheap and
; being a copy-and-paste hazard.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PSSET2: LD      (VPST+1),HL
        LD      (VPIN+1),DE
        RET
PSSETO: LD      (VPOST+1),HL
        LD      (VPOUT+1),DE
        RET
PSSET4: LD      (VPST+1),HL     ; all four at once, for the null stubs
        LD      (VPIN+1),HL
        LD      (VPOST+1),HL
        LD      (VPOUT+1),HL
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PNULL - the no-port-selected stub.  Reports "never ready, never sends"
; rather than reading a port that may not exist: on real hardware an IN
; from an unpopulated address returns floating bus noise, which would
; look like incoming data.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PNULL:  XOR     A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Z80 SIO/2 driver.  PBASE holds the control/status port; data is the
; next address up, which is how every RC2014-class SIO/2 board is wired.
; RR0: bit 0 = receive character available, bit 2 = transmit buffer
; empty.  B is cleared before IN A,(C) because the Z80 puts BC on the
; address bus and stray high bits confuse partially-decoded boards.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SIOST:  LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        IN      A,(C)
        AND     01H
        RET
SIOIN:  LD      A,(PBASE)
        INC     A
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        IN      A,(C)
        RET
SIOOST: LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        IN      A,(C)
        AND     04H
        RET
SIOOUT: LD      E,A             ; hold the byte; A is needed for the port
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        INC     A
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        LD      A,E
        OUT     (C),A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; 6850 ACIA driver (Altair 88-2SIO, RC2014 ACIA boards, and many others).
; PBASE holds the control/status port, data the next address up.  Status:
; bit 0 = receive data register full, bit 1 = transmit data register
; empty.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ACIST:  LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        IN      A,(C)
        AND     01H
        RET
ACIIN:  LD      A,(PBASE)
        INC     A
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        IN      A,(C)
        RET
ACIOST: LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        IN      A,(C)
        AND     02H
        RET
ACIOUT: LD      E,A
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        INC     A
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        LD      A,E
        OUT     (C),A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ACINIT - a 6850 will not talk until it is programmed, and unlike the
; SIO it forgets nothing gracefully: a master reset (03H) followed by
; 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit at the /16 clock divisor (15H).
; Done when the port is selected rather than at startup, so choosing a
; different family never writes to a chip the user did not pick.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ACINIT: LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        LD      B,0
        LD      A,03H           ; master reset
        OUT     (C),A
        LD      A,15H           ; 8N1, /16 clock, receive interrupt off
        OUT     (C),A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; RomWBW HBIOS driver.  No port at all: the firmware moves the byte.  A
; call is RST 8 with the function in B and the unit in C, and the result
; comes back in A with the flags set from it - which is exactly the
; convention the vectors here want, so the status calls need no masking.
; PBASE holds the unit number.
;
; Only usable on a RomWBW machine (or the gateway's emulator with an
; hbios profile); SCPHB checks for the RST 8 vector before selecting it,
; because on a bare CP/M machine RST 8 lands in unused memory.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
HBST:   LD      B,02H           ; serial input status
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        RST     8
        RET
HBIN:   LD      B,00H           ; serial input (waits for a character)
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        RST     8
        LD      A,E             ; the byte arrives in E
        RET
HBOST:  LD      B,03H           ; serial output status
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        RST     8
        RET
HBOUT:  LD      E,A             ; the byte goes out in E
        LD      B,01H           ; serial output
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        LD      C,A
        RST     8
        RET


;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Z180 ASCI driver (SC126 and other Z180 boards).
;
; The ASCI registers are Z180 *internal* I/O, reached with IN0/OUT0 -
; instructions a Z80 assembler cannot encode, so they are laid down as
; DB ED,38,port (IN0 A,(port)) and DB ED,39,port (OUT0 (port),A).  The
; operand is an immediate byte, so ASCPAT writes the addresses into these
; instructions when the port is selected: the same self-patching the
; vectors use, one level down.  Every such instruction is labelled so the
; patcher names what it is writing to rather than counting offsets.
;
; Register layout, relative to the internal I/O base (PABASE, 00H unless
; the system has moved it) - channel 1 is the same +1:
;
;   CNTLA0 = base+0    STAT0 = base+4    TDR0 = base+6    RDR0 = base+8
;
; STAT: bit 7 RDRF, bit 6 OVRN, bit 5 PE, bit 4 FE, bit 1 TDRE.
; CNTLA: bit 3 EFR - writing a 0 there resets the three error flags.
;
; Baud rate and framing are deliberately left alone: they live in CNTLA
; and CNTLB, the system set them, and a terminal has no business
; re-timing a port that is already working.  The only write to CNTLA here
; is the error reset, and it is read-modify-write so nothing else changes.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ASCST:  DB      0EDH,38H
ASCS1:  DB      00H             ; IN0 A,(STATn)  - operand patched
        LD      E,A
        AND     70H             ; OVRN, PE or FE latched?
        JR      NZ,ASCERR
        LD      A,E
        AND     80H             ; RDRF
        RET
ASCIN:  DB      0EDH,38H
ASCR1:  DB      00H             ; IN0 A,(RDRn)
        RET
ASCOST: DB      0EDH,38H
ASCS2:  DB      00H             ; IN0 A,(STATn)
        AND     02H             ; TDRE
        RET
ASCOUT: DB      0EDH,39H
ASCT1:  DB      00H             ; OUT0 (TDRn),A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ASCERR - a receive error is latched.  This matters more than it looks:
; on the Z180 an overrun stops the receiver until the error is reset, so
; a single burst of line noise would otherwise leave the port deaf for
; the rest of the session with no indication of why.
;
; The byte that arrived with the error is discarded rather than handed up
; - it is known bad, and a protocol is better served by a missing byte it
; will ask for again than by a corrupt one it has to detect.  Then the
; flags are reset and "nothing to read" is reported.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ASCERR: LD      A,E
        AND     80H             ; did a byte arrive with the error?
        JR      Z,ASCER1
        DB      0EDH,38H
ASCR2:  DB      00H             ; IN0 A,(RDRn) - discard the suspect byte
ASCER1: DB      0EDH,38H
ASCC1:  DB      00H             ; IN0 A,(CNTLAn)
        AND     0F7H            ; EFR = 0 resets OVRN/PE/FE
        DB      0EDH,39H
ASCC2:  DB      00H             ; OUT0 (CNTLAn),A
        XOR     A               ; report: nothing readable this time
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ASCPAT - write the register addresses into the ASCI routines.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ASCPAT: LD      A,(PCHAN)
        AND     01H             ; channel 0 or 1
        LD      E,A
        LD      A,(PABASE)      ; internal I/O base (00H unless moved)
        ADD     A,E
        LD      D,A             ; D = base + channel
        LD      A,D
        ADD     A,04H           ; STATn
        LD      (ASCS1),A
        LD      (ASCS2),A
        LD      A,D
        ADD     A,08H           ; RDRn
        LD      (ASCR1),A
        LD      (ASCR2),A
        LD      A,D
        ADD     A,06H           ; TDRn
        LD      (ASCT1),A
        LD      A,D             ; CNTLAn is the base itself
        LD      (ASCC1),A
        LD      (ASCC2),A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ISZ180 - is this processor a Z180?  Carry set if it is not.
;
; The ASCI lives inside the CPU, so selecting it on a plain Z80 does not
; just fail - IN0/OUT0 are undefined there, and the driver would read
; whatever an undefined opcode leaves behind.  Better to know first.
;
; MLT is the convenient test: on a Z180, ED 4C multiplies B by C into BC;
; on a Z80 that opcode is undefined and behaves as a two-byte no-op, so
; BC comes back untouched.  2 x 2 = 4 separates the two with no side
; effects on either.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
ISZ180: LD      BC,0202H
        DB      0EDH,4CH        ; MLT BC on a Z180; no-op on a Z80
        LD      A,B
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,ISZNOT       ; B still 2: not a Z180
        LD      A,C
        CP      04H             ; 2 x 2
        JR      NZ,ISZNOT
        XOR     A               ; carry clear: this is a Z180
        RET
ISZNOT: SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CP/M AUX device driver (BDOS functions 3 and 4).
;
; The portable fallback: no hardware knowledge at all, so it works
; wherever the operating system has an AUX device - including the
; gateway's emulator, where AUX is wired to the virtual modem.
;
; CP/M 2.2 has NO way to ask whether an AUX byte is waiting, which is the
; catch.  The status call therefore READS one byte and holds it: the
; gateway returns ^Z when nothing is waiting, so ^Z means "not ready" and
; anything else is stashed for the next read.  That makes polling work
; here at the price of not being able to receive a literal ^Z on this
; family, and on real CP/M 2.2 hardware - where BDOS 3 blocks until a
; byte arrives - the status call waits rather than returning.  Both are
; consequences of the missing status call, not choices; use a hardware
; family instead when one fits the machine.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
AUXST:  LD      A,(AUXFLG)      ; already holding one?
        OR      A
        RET     NZ
        PUSH    BC
        PUSH    DE
        PUSH    HL
        LD      C,03H           ; BDOS 3: AUX input
        CALL    BDOS
        POP     HL
        POP     DE
        POP     BC
        CP      1AH             ; ^Z = nothing waiting
        JR      Z,AUXST0
        LD      (AUXHLD),A
        LD      A,1
        LD      (AUXFLG),A
        RET                     ; A is non-zero: ready
AUXST0: XOR     A
        RET
AUXIN:  CALL    AUXST           ; make sure a byte is held
        XOR     A
        LD      (AUXFLG),A
        LD      A,(AUXHLD)
        RET
AUXOST: LD      A,1             ; the BDOS blocks for us: always ready
        RET
AUXOUT: PUSH    BC
        PUSH    DE
        PUSH    HL
        LD      E,A
        LD      C,04H           ; BDOS 4: AUX output
        CALL    BDOS
        POP     HL
        POP     DE
        POP     BC
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Port vector call sites.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PST:    JP      VPST
PIN:    JP      VPIN
POST:   JP      VPOST
POUT:   JP      VPOUT

;=======================================================================
; PSEND - send the byte in A out the port, waiting for room.
;
; The wait is BOUNDED, and that matters more than it sounds.  If the user
; has picked the wrong port, the transmitter is never ready - an absent
; chip answers every read the same way forever - so an unbounded wait
; hangs the program at the first keystroke, with the menu key dead
; because the loop never gets back to reading the keyboard.  That is the
; exact moment a user needs the menu, so instead the send gives up, sets
; PDEAD, and terminal mode reports what happened and hands back control.
;
; The bound is deliberately generous - four passes of a 65536-poll loop,
; several seconds on a 4 MHz Z80 - because a *working* port can legally
; stall for a long time when hardware flow control is holding it off.
; Diagnosing a dead port must not misfire on a slow peer.
;
; The counter lives in BC and is saved around the status call, because
; the port drivers use BC for the I/O address.
;=======================================================================
PSEND:  PUSH    BC              ; BC is this routine's poll counter; callers
        LD      (SENDCH),A      ; count block bytes in B, so hand it back
        LD      A,4
        LD      (PTRIES),A
PSNDO:  LD      BC,0            ; 65536 polls per pass
PSEND1: PUSH    BC
        CALL    POST
        POP     BC
        JR      NZ,PSEND2
        DEC     BC
        LD      A,B
        OR      C
        JR      NZ,PSEND1
        LD      A,(PTRIES)
        DEC     A
        LD      (PTRIES),A
        JR      NZ,PSNDO
        POP     BC
        LD      A,1
        LD      (PDEAD),A       ; transmitter never came free
        RET
PSEND2: LD      A,(SENDCH)
        CALL    POUT            ; the driver clobbers BC, so restore AFTER it
        POP     BC
        RET

;=======================================================================
; MAIN MENU
;=======================================================================
MAINLP: CALL    SCMENU
        CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      'T'
        JR      Z,DOTERM
        CP      'P'
        JR      Z,DOPORT
        CP      'S'
        JR      Z,DOSETT
        CP      'U'
        JR      Z,DOSEND
        CP      'D'
        JR      Z,DORECV
        CP      'H'
        JR      Z,DOHELP
        CP      'V'
        JR      Z,DOSAVE
        CP      'X'
        JR      Z,DOEXIT
        LD      A,BEL           ; unknown key: a beep, no scolding
        CALL    COUT
        JR      MAINLP
DOTERM: CALL    TERM
        JR      MAINLP
DOPORT: CALL    SCPORT
        JR      MAINLP
DOSETT: CALL    SCSETT
        JR      MAINLP
DOHELP: CALL    SCHELP
        JR      MAINLP
DOSAVE: CALL    SAVCFG
        JR      MAINLP
DOSEND: CALL    XSEND
        CALL    TWAIT
        JR      MAINLP
DORECV: CALL    XRECV
        CALL    TWAIT
        JR      MAINLP
DOEXIT: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MBYE
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      WBOOT

;=======================================================================
; TERMINAL MODE
;
; The hot loop.  Port first, then console: incoming data is the side that
; loses bytes if it is left waiting, while a human's keystroke can spare
; the microseconds.
;=======================================================================
TERM:   LD      A,(PKIND)
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,TERM1
        LD      HL,MNOPRT       ; refuse rather than read a dead port
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      TWAIT
TERM1:  CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MTERM
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHESC           ; name the escape key in the banner
        LD      HL,MTERM2
        CALL    PSTR
        XOR     A
        LD      (FSTATE),A      ; no half-finished escape sequence
        LD      (ESTATE),A      ; on either stream
        LD      (PDEAD),A       ; and no diagnosis pending
TLOOP:  CALL    PST             ; anything from the port?
        JR      Z,TL1
        CALL    PIN
        CALL    FILT            ; filter, then to the console
TL1:    CALL    CST             ; anything from the keyboard?
        JR      Z,TLOOP
        CALL    CIN
        LD      HL,ESCCH
        CP      (HL)            ; the escape key?
        JR      Z,TESC
        CALL    TSEND
        LD      A,(PDEAD)
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,TDEAD
        JP      TLOOP

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; TDEAD - the port never accepted the byte.  Name the port, say what it
; means, and leave terminal mode so the menu is reachable again.  This is
; the failure every other terminal here presents as a silent hang.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
TDEAD:  CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MDEAD
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHPORT
        LD      HL,MDEAD2
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      TWAIT

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; TSEND - send a typed character, honouring local echo and CR handling.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
TSEND:  LD      (TYPECH),A
        LD      A,(ECHOFL)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,TSEND1
        LD      A,(TYPECH)
        CALL    FILTE           ; echo through its own filter state
TSEND1: LD      A,(TYPECH)
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,(TYPECH)
        CP      CR
        RET     NZ
        LD      A,(CRLFFL)      ; append LF to a typed CR?
        OR      A
        RET     Z
        LD      A,(ECHOFL)      ; if the echo is ours, echo the LF too -
        OR      A               ; otherwise the local screen keeps
        JR      Z,TSEND2        ; overwriting the same line
        LD      A,LF
        CALL    FILTE
TSEND2: LD      A,LF
        JP      PSEND

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; TESC - the escape key was pressed in terminal mode.
;
; One keystroke decides: leave, get help, or send the escape character
; itself (pressing it twice), so the key is never trapped away from the
; remote end.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
TESC:   CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MESCMN
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        LD      HL,ESCCH        ; escape twice: send it literally
        CP      (HL)
        JR      Z,TESCLI
        CP      'E'
        JR      Z,TESCEX
        CP      'H'
        JR      Z,TESCHL
        CP      'S'
        JR      Z,TESCST
        CP      'U'
        JR      Z,TESCU
        CP      'D'
        JR      Z,TESCD
        CALL    CRLF            ; anything else: back to the session
        JP      TLOOP
TESCLI: LD      A,(ESCCH)
        CALL    PSEND
        JP      TLOOP
TESCHL: CALL    SCHELP
        JP      TLOOP
TESCST: CALL    SCSETT
        JP      TLOOP
TESCU:  CALL    XSEND           ; transfer without leaving the session
        CALL    TWAIT
        JP      TLOOP
TESCD:  CALL    XRECV
        CALL    TWAIT
        JP      TLOOP
TESCEX: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MTEXIT
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; TWAIT - "press a key" pause, for screens reached from a menu.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
TWAIT:  LD      HL,MKEY
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    CIN
        CALL    CRLF
        RET

;=======================================================================
; FILT / FILTE - filter a byte to the console.  Byte in A.
;
; FILT  is the INBOUND path (bytes from the port).
; FILTE is the LOCAL ECHO path (bytes the user just typed).
;
; They are the same code over SEPARATE state bytes, and that separation
; matters: the state machine spans several bytes of an escape sequence, so
; a single shared state would let a typed ESC swallow the next byte that
; arrived from the port - two independent streams cannot share one
; half-finished sequence.  HL carries the state byte's address through the
; whole routine, which is why nothing here may clobber it.
;
; ANSI mode passes everything through, for a terminal that understands
; escape sequences.  ASCII mode is for a printing terminal or a console
; with no cursor addressing: the high bit is stripped (the classic
; parity-bit strip, so 8-bit text reads as ASCII rather than as garbage),
; CSI sequences are swallowed whole, DEL is dropped, and of the control
; codes only CR, LF, TAB, BS and BEL - the ones that do useful work on any
; terminal - are let through.
;
; State: 0 = normal, 1 = an ESC was seen, 2 = inside a CSI sequence.  The MODE
; is tested before the state, and ANSI clears it: otherwise switching from
; ASCII to ANSI part-way through a sequence would keep swallowing bytes on the
; new setting until that sequence happened to finish.
;=======================================================================
FILT:   LD      HL,FSTATE       ; inbound stream's own state
        JR      FILT0
FILTE:  LD      HL,ESTATE       ; echo stream's own state
FILT0:  LD      E,A             ; hold the byte
        LD      A,(FLTMOD)
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,FASCII
        LD      (HL),0          ; ANSI: no sequence state to carry
        LD      A,E
        JP      COUT            ; straight through
FASCII: LD      A,(HL)          ; ASCII: mid-sequence?
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,FILT2
        LD      A,E
        AND     7FH             ; strip the high bit
        LD      E,A
        CP      ESC
        JR      Z,FSESC
        CP      DEL
        RET     Z               ; DEL prints as litter: drop it
        CP      20H
        JR      NC,FPRIN        ; printable
        CP      CR
        JR      Z,FPRIN
        CP      LF
        JR      Z,FPRIN
        CP      TAB
        JR      Z,FPRIN
        CP      BS
        JR      Z,FPRIN
        CP      BEL
        JR      Z,FPRIN
        RET                     ; other control code: drop it
FPRIN:  JP      COUT
FSESC:  LD      (HL),1
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; FILT2 - mid-sequence.  After ESC, '[' opens a CSI (swallow until its
; final byte, 40H-7EH); anything else is a two-character sequence, so it
; and the ESC are both gone.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
FILT2:  DEC     A
        JR      NZ,FCSI         ; state 2: already inside a CSI
        LD      A,E
        AND     7FH
        CP      '['
        JR      Z,FCSIST
        LD      (HL),0          ; two-character escape: done
        RET
FCSIST: LD      (HL),2
        RET
FCSI:   LD      A,E
        AND     7FH
        CP      40H
        RET     C               ; parameter byte: keep swallowing
        CP      7FH
        RET     NC
        LD      (HL),0          ; final byte: sequence complete
        RET



;=======================================================================
; XMODEM
;
; Plain XMODEM: 128-byte blocks, CRC-16 with the checksum fallback a
; vintage peer may insist on.  One 128-byte buffer serves as both the
; protocol block and the CP/M record, because they are the same size -
; that is why XMODEM and CP/M fit together so neatly, and it is what
; keeps the memory cost of transfers to a single buffer.
;
; The CRC is computed a bit at a time rather than from a 512-byte table.
; A table would cost more memory than the whole transfer code and buy
; nothing: at 9600 baud a byte takes a millisecond and the CRC of one
; takes microseconds, so the wire is the limit, not the arithmetic.
;
; Both directions run from the menu with the connection already up, so
; the usual sequence is: dial in terminal mode, tell the far end what you
; want, then press U or D here.  Leaving terminal mode does not hang up.
;=======================================================================

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XSEND - send a file (upload to the far end).
;
; The receiver drives the start: it sends 'C' for CRC mode or NAK for
; checksum, repeatedly, and we answer whichever arrives.  That is the
; protocol's one piece of negotiation, and honouring it is what makes a
; transfer work against both a modern peer and a 1980s one.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XSEND:  CALL    XREADY          ; is a port selected at all?
        RET     C
        CALL    RSTDMA          ; start from CP/M's own record buffer
        LD      HL,MXSFIL
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    XFNAME
        RET     C
        LD      DE,XFCB         ; must exist to be sent
        LD      C,BOPEN
        CALL    BDOS
        INC     A
        JR      NZ,XSEND1
        LD      HL,MXNOFIL
        CALL    PSTR
        RET
XSEND1: LD      A,XNOISE
        LD      (XNCNT),A
        LD      HL,MXWAIT
        CALL    PSTR
        ; No XPURGE here on purpose.  The receiver is already sending its
        ; 'C' every few seconds, and a purge window on a slow machine can
        ; be long enough to swallow each one as it arrives - the sender
        ; would then wait out the whole negotiation with the answer in its
        ; hand.  The handshake loop below tolerates noise instead.
        LD      A,XRETRY
        LD      (XCNT),A
XSEND2: CALL    XCHKAB          ; waiting for the far end is the likeliest
        JP      C,XUSER         ; moment to change your mind
        LD      A,XLONG         ; wait for the receiver to ask
        LD      (XTMO),A
        CALL    XGETB
        JR      NC,XSEND3
        LD      A,(XCNT)        ; nothing yet: keep waiting a while
        DEC     A
        LD      (XCNT),A
        JR      NZ,XSEND2
        JP      XFAIL
XSEND3: CP      CRCC
        JR      Z,XSENDC
        CP      NAK
        JR      Z,XSENDK
        CP      CAN
        JP      Z,XABORT
        ; Noise.  Not an error: the far end usually prints something like
        ; "start your transfer now" first, and every character of it
        ; arrives here.  So it gets its own generous budget, kept apart
        ; from the retry count - spending a retry per character of a
        ; banner would fail the transfer before it began, which is
        ; exactly what an earlier version of this did.
        LD      A,(XNCNT)
        DEC     A
        LD      (XNCNT),A
        JR      NZ,XSEND2
        JP      XFAIL
XSENDC: LD      A,1             ; CRC mode
        JR      XSEND4
XSENDK: XOR     A               ; checksum mode
XSEND4: LD      (XCRCM),A
        CALL    XPURGE          ; drop the far end's "start now" banner: from
                                ; here on, anything arriving is protocol
        LD      A,1
        LD      (XBLK),A        ; XMODEM blocks start at 1
        LD      HL,MXGO
        CALL    PSTR

;--- the per-record loop: read one CP/M record, send it, wait for ACK ---
XSLOOP: CALL    XCHKAB          ; a keypress can stop this
        JP      C,XUSER
        LD      DE,XBUF
        LD      C,BSETDMA
        CALL    BDOS
        LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BREADS
        CALL    BDOS
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,XSDONE       ; no more records: end of file
        LD      A,XRETRY
        LD      (XCNT),A
XSBLK:  CALL    XBLKSN          ; put the block on the wire
XSWAIT: LD      A,XLONG
        LD      (XTMO),A
        CALL    XGETB
        JR      C,XSBAD         ; silence: send the block again
        CP      ACK
        JR      Z,XSNEXT
        CP      CAN
        JP      Z,XABORT
        CP      NAK
        JR      Z,XSBAD         ; asked for it again
        LD      A,(XNCNT)       ; anything else is noise: keep waiting for a
        DEC     A               ; real answer instead of resending on it
        LD      (XNCNT),A
        JR      NZ,XSWAIT
        JP      XFAIL
XSBAD:  LD      A,'?'           ; resend this block
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,(XCNT)
        DEC     A
        LD      (XCNT),A
        JR      NZ,XSBLK
        JP      XFAIL
XSNEXT: LD      A,'.'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      HL,XBLK
        INC     (HL)            ; block numbers wrap at 256, as they must
        JR      XSLOOP

;--- end of file: EOT until acknowledged, then tidy up ---
XSDONE: LD      A,XRETRY
        LD      (XCNT),A
XSEOT:  LD      A,EOT
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,XLONG
        LD      (XTMO),A
        CALL    XGETB
        JR      C,XSEOT1
        CP      ACK
        JR      Z,XSOK
XSEOT1: LD      A,(XCNT)
        DEC     A
        LD      (XCNT),A
        JR      NZ,XSEOT
        JP      XFAIL
XSOK:   LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BCLOSE
        CALL    BDOS
        CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXSENT
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XBLKSN - send the block in XBUF: header, 128 bytes, then the check.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XBLKSN: LD      A,SOH
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,(XBLK)
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,(XBLK)
        CPL                     ; the header carries the block number twice,
        CALL    PSEND           ; the second time inverted
        LD      HL,0            ; CRC accumulator
        LD      (XCRC),HL
        XOR     A
        LD      (XSUM),A        ; checksum accumulator
        LD      HL,XBUF
        LD      B,128
XBLKS1: LD      A,(HL)
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,(HL)
        CALL    XACCUM          ; fold the byte into both checks
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        JR      NZ,XBLKS1
        LD      A,(XCRCM)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,XBLKS2
        LD      HL,(XCRC)       ; CRC-16, high byte first
        LD      A,H
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,L
        JP      PSEND
XBLKS2: LD      A,(XSUM)
        JP      PSEND

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XRECV - receive a file (download from the far end).
;
; We drive the start here: 'C' every couple of seconds to ask for CRC
; mode, and if the far end never answers, NAK for the rest of the
; attempts - a peer too old to know CRC mode responds to that instead.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XRECV:  CALL    XREADY
        RET     C
        CALL    RSTDMA          ; the directory search below uses the DMA
        LD      HL,MXRFIL
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    XFNAME
        RET     C
        LD      DE,XFCB         ; refuse to clobber silently
        LD      C,BSEARF
        CALL    BDOS
        INC     A
        JR      Z,XRECV1
        LD      HL,MXEXIST
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      'Y'
        JR      Z,XRECVD
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXKEEP
        CALL    PSTR
        RET
XRECVD: LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BDELET
        CALL    BDOS
XRECV1: LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BMAKE
        CALL    BDOS
        INC     A
        JR      NZ,XRECV2
        LD      HL,MXNOMK       ; directory full, or a bad name
        CALL    PSTR
        RET
XRECV2: LD      A,1
        LD      (XRXACT),A      ; from here a partial file exists on disk
        LD      A,XNOISE
        LD      (XNCNT),A
        LD      A,1
        LD      (XCRCM),A       ; ask for CRC mode first
        LD      A,1
        LD      (XBLK),A
        LD      HL,MXGO
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    XPURGE
        LD      A,XRETRY+XRETRY ; 'C' attempts, then NAK attempts
        LD      (XCNT),A

;--- ask for a block, then take whatever arrives ---
XRLOOP: LD      A,(XCRCM)
        OR      A
        LD      A,CRCC
        JR      NZ,XRL1
        LD      A,NAK
XRL1:   CALL    PSEND
XRWAIT: CALL    XCHKAB
        JP      C,XUSER
        LD      A,XLONG
        LD      (XTMO),A
        CALL    XGETB
        JR      NC,XRGOT
        LD      A,(XCNT)        ; silence: ask again, and after half the
        DEC     A               ; attempts stop asking for CRC mode
        LD      (XCNT),A
        JP      Z,XFAIL
        CP      XRETRY
        JR      NZ,XRLOOP
        XOR     A
        LD      (XCRCM),A       ; fall back to checksum
        JR      XRLOOP
XRGOT:  CP      SOH
        JR      Z,XRBLK
        CP      EOT
        JP      Z,XRDONE
        CP      CAN
        JP      Z,XABORT
        LD      A,(XNCNT)       ; noise: same generous, separate budget
        DEC     A
        LD      (XNCNT),A
        JP      Z,XFAIL
        JP      XRWAIT

;--- a block header arrived: read it, check it, write it ---
XRBLK:  LD      A,XSHORT        ; the rest of a block must arrive promptly
        LD      (XTMO),A
        CALL    XGETB
        JP      C,XRBAD
        LD      (XGOTBL),A      ; block number as sent
        CALL    XGETB
        JP      C,XRBAD
        LD      HL,XGOTBL       ; ...and its complement must agree
        CPL
        CP      (HL)
        JP      NZ,XRBAD
        LD      HL,0
        LD      (XCRC),HL
        XOR     A
        LD      (XSUM),A
        LD      HL,XBUF
        LD      B,128
XRB1:   CALL    XGETB
        JR      C,XRBAD
        LD      (HL),A
        CALL    XACCUM
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        JR      NZ,XRB1
        LD      A,(XCRCM)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,XRB2
        CALL    XGETB           ; CRC-16: two bytes, high first
        JR      C,XRBAD
        LD      HL,(XCRC)
        CP      H
        JR      NZ,XRBAD
        CALL    XGETB
        JR      C,XRBAD
        LD      HL,(XCRC)
        CP      L
        JR      NZ,XRBAD
        JR      XRGOOD
XRB2:   CALL    XGETB
        JR      C,XRBAD
        LD      HL,XSUM
        CP      (HL)
        JR      NZ,XRBAD

;--- the block is sound: is it the one we expect? ---
XRGOOD: LD      HL,XBLK
        LD      A,(XGOTBL)
        CP      (HL)
        JR      Z,XRSAVE
        ; Not the expected block.  If it is the PREVIOUS one, the sender
        ; never saw our ACK and has resent it: acknowledge again and do
        ; NOT write it a second time.  Anything else means the two ends
        ; have lost step, which is a bad block.
        ;
        ; Note the register work: the comparison result has to survive
        ; until the branch, so "expected minus one" is computed in A
        ; rather than by DEC/CP/INC on the counter in memory - INC (HL)
        ; sets the flags itself and would overwrite the very answer the
        ; branch is waiting for.
        LD      A,(HL)
        DEC     A               ; the block before the one we want
        LD      HL,XGOTBL
        CP      (HL)
        JR      NZ,XRBAD        ; a different block entirely: out of step
        LD      A,ACK
        CALL    PSEND
        JP      XRRSET
XRSAVE: LD      DE,XBUF
        LD      C,BSETDMA
        CALL    BDOS
        LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BWRITS
        CALL    BDOS
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,XRDISK       ; disk full: stop rather than lose data
        LD      A,ACK
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,'.'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      HL,XBLK
        INC     (HL)
XRRSET: LD      A,XRETRY        ; progress: a fresh budget of retries
        LD      (XCNT),A
        LD      A,XNOISE        ; ...and of tolerance for junk
        LD      (XNCNT),A
        JP      XRWAIT

;--- a bad block: drain the line, NAK, try again ---
XRBAD:  LD      A,'?'
        CALL    COUT
        CALL    XPURGE
        LD      A,(XCNT)
        DEC     A
        LD      (XCNT),A
        JP      Z,XFAIL
        LD      A,NAK
        CALL    PSEND
        JP      XRWAIT

;--- EOT: acknowledge, close, done ---
XRDONE: LD      A,ACK
        CALL    PSEND
        XOR     A
        LD      (XRXACT),A      ; complete: it is a real file now
        LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BCLOSE
        CALL    BDOS
        CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXRECVD
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

XRDISK: CALL    XCANC
        CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXDISK
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      XSCRAP           ; deleting it also gives the space back

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XACCUM - fold the byte in A into both the CRC and the checksum, so the
; block loops need not care which check mode is in force.
;
; CRC-16 with polynomial 1021H, one bit at a time: the byte goes into the
; high half of the accumulator, then eight shifts, XORing the polynomial
; back in whenever a 1 falls off the top.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XACCUM: PUSH    BC              ; the block loops count 128 bytes in B and
        PUSH    HL              ; walk the buffer with HL; this routine needs
        LD      E,A             ; both, so it borrows and gives them back
        LD      HL,XSUM
        ADD     A,(HL)
        LD      (HL),A          ; checksum: a plain 8-bit sum
        LD      HL,(XCRC)
        LD      A,H
        XOR     E
        LD      H,A
        LD      B,8
XACC1:  ADD     HL,HL
        JR      NC,XACC2
        LD      A,H
        XOR     10H
        LD      H,A
        LD      A,L
        XOR     21H
        LD      L,A
XACC2:  DEC     B
        JR      NZ,XACC1
        LD      (XCRC),HL
        POP     HL
        POP     BC
        LD      A,E
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XGETB - wait for a byte from the port.  Byte in A, carry clear; carry
; set if nothing came within (XTMO) passes of a 65536-poll loop.
;
; A poll loop is the only clock a CP/M program can count on - there is no
; timer service in CP/M 2.2 - so the wait is measured in polls, which
; makes it faster on a faster machine.  That is acceptable here: both
; bounds are generous multiples of the time a byte actually takes, and the
; retry counts do the real work of bounding a transfer.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XGETB:  PUSH    BC              ; BC is this routine's poll counter, and the
        LD      A,(XTMO)        ; block loops are counting in B - hand it back
        LD      (XTMOC),A
XGETB0: LD      BC,0
XGETB1: PUSH    BC
        CALL    PST
        POP     BC
        JR      NZ,XGETB2
        DEC     BC
        LD      A,B
        OR      C
        JR      NZ,XGETB1
        LD      A,(XTMOC)
        DEC     A
        LD      (XTMOC),A
        JR      NZ,XGETB0
        POP     BC
        SCF                     ; nothing arrived
        RET
XGETB2: CALL    PIN
        POP     BC
        OR      A               ; carry clear: a byte is in A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XPURGE - swallow whatever is still coming until the line goes quiet,
; so a retry starts from a known state instead of half-way through the
; block that just failed.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XPURGE: LD      A,XSHORT
        LD      (XTMO),A
        CALL    XGETB
        JR      NC,XPURGE
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XREADY - refuse a transfer when no port is selected, with the same
; wording the terminal uses, rather than "transferring" into nothing.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XREADY: LD      A,(PKIND)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,XREADY1
        XOR     A               ; a fresh diagnosis for this transfer: the
        LD      (PDEAD),A       ; flag may be left over from a past session
        RET                     ; carry clear: good to go
XREADY1: CALL   CRLF
        LD      HL,MNOPRT
        CALL    PSTR
        SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XCANC / XABORT / XFAIL - the ways a transfer ends badly.  Two CANs are
; how XMODEM says "stop"; one can be line noise, which is why the
; protocol asks for two and why we send two.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XCANC:  LD      A,CAN
        CALL    PSEND
        LD      A,CAN
        JP      PSEND

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XSCRAP - throw away a partly received file.
;
; A download that stops early has written real records to a real file:
; the directory entry exists and its contents are a prefix of the truth.
; Leaving that on disk is the worst outcome, because it looks like a
; file - the same silent-truncation trap the gateway's own Kermit
; receiver refuses.  So an incomplete download is deleted, and the user
; is told.  XRXACT marks the window in which an output file exists;
; nothing is deleted outside it, so a failed *upload* never touches the
; file it was reading.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XSCRAP: LD      A,(XRXACT)
        OR      A
        RET     Z               ; no download in progress: nothing to undo
        XOR     A
        LD      (XRXACT),A
        LD      DE,XFCB
        LD      C,BDELET
        CALL    BDOS
        LD      HL,MXSCRAP
        JP      PSTR
XABORT: CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXCAN
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      XSCRAP
XFAIL:  CALL    XCANC
        CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      A,(PDEAD)       ; did the PORT stop taking characters?
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,XFAILP
        LD      HL,MXFAIL
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      XSCRAP
XFAILP: LD      HL,MDEAD        ; then give the port diagnosis, not a vague
        CALL    PSTR            ; "the other end stopped answering"
        CALL    SHPORT
        LD      HL,MDEAD2
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      XSCRAP

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XCHKAB - has the user asked to stop?  Carry set if so.
;
; Checked once per block rather than per byte: a block is milliseconds of
; work, and this way the cost is nothing while the transfer still answers
; a keypress.  Without it, a transfer whose peer has walked away can only
; be waited out through every retry - which is the one moment a person is
; most likely to want out.
;
; ESC or the menu key stop it; any other key is swallowed, because a
; stray keystroke during a transfer should not end it.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XCHKAB: CALL    CST
        RET     Z               ; nothing typed: carry clear
        CALL    CIN
        CP      ESC
        JR      Z,XCHKA1
        LD      HL,ESCCH
        CP      (HL)
        JR      Z,XCHKA1
        XOR     A               ; some other key: ignore it
        RET
XCHKA1: SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XUSER - the user stopped it.  Tell the far end with the protocol's own
; cancel, then clean up exactly as a failure does.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XUSER:  CALL    XCANC
        CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXUSER
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      XSCRAP

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XFNAME - prompt for a filename and build an FCB from it.
;
; Accepts an optional drive letter (B:PROG.COM), folds to upper case, and
; pads the name and type with spaces the way CP/M expects.  Carry set if
; the user typed nothing, so RETURN always means "never mind".
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XFNAME: LD      A,20            ; room for drive + 8.3 + slack
        LD      (XLINE),A
        LD      DE,XLINE
        LD      C,BRDBUF
        CALL    BDOS
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      A,(XLINE+1)     ; how many characters were typed
        OR      A
        JP      Z,XFNBAD
        LD      B,A
        LD      HL,XLINE+2
        XOR     A               ; clear the FCB: drive 0, spaces, no extent
        LD      (XFCB),A
        LD      DE,XFCB+1
        LD      A,' '
        LD      C,11
XFN1:   LD      (DE),A
        INC     DE
        DEC     C
        JR      NZ,XFN1
        XOR     A
        LD      (XFCB+12),A
        LD      (XFCB+13),A
        LD      (XFCB+14),A
        LD      (XFCB+15),A
        LD      (XFCB+32),A     ; current record
        ; a drive letter?
        LD      A,B
        CP      2
        JR      C,XFN2
        LD      A,(XLINE+3)
        CP      ':'
        JR      NZ,XFN2
        LD      A,(XLINE+2)
        CALL    UPCASE
        SUB     'A'-1           ; FCB drive is 1-based (1 = A:)
        LD      (XFCB),A
        INC     HL
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        DEC     B
        ; name, up to 8 characters, then the type after a dot
XFN2:   LD      DE,XFCB+1
        LD      C,8
XFN3:   LD      A,B
        OR      A
        JR      Z,XFNOK
        LD      A,(HL)
        CP      '.'
        JR      Z,XFN4
        CALL    XWILD           ; one file, named exactly
        JP      C,XFNBAD
        CALL    UPCASE
        LD      (DE),A
        INC     DE
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        DEC     C
        JR      NZ,XFN3
XFN3A:  LD      A,B             ; name full: skip to the dot
        OR      A
        JR      Z,XFNOK
        LD      A,(HL)
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        CP      '.'
        JR      NZ,XFN3A
XFN4:   LD      A,(HL)          ; step over the dot itself
        CP      '.'
        JR      NZ,XFN5
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
XFN5:   LD      DE,XFCB+9
        LD      C,3
XFN6:   LD      A,B
        OR      A
        JR      Z,XFNOK
        LD      A,(HL)
        CALL    XWILD
        JP      C,XFNBAD
        CALL    UPCASE
        LD      (DE),A
        INC     DE
        INC     HL
        DEC     B
        DEC     C
        JR      NZ,XFN6
XFNOK:  LD      A,(XFCB+1)      ; a name of nothing but spaces is no name
        CP      ' '
        JR      Z,XFNBAD
        XOR     A               ; carry clear: the FCB is ready
        RET
XFNBAD: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MXNONAM
        CALL    PSTR
        SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; XWILD - carry set if the character in A is a wildcard.
;
; XMODEM moves one file with no name of its own, so '*' and '?' have
; nothing to match against: passed into an FCB they would open some
; arbitrary file to send, or create a file literally called '?' on a
; receive.  Refusing them is kinder than either.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
XWILD:  CP      '*'
        JR      Z,XWILD1
        CP      '?'
        JR      Z,XWILD1
        RET                     ; carry clear from CP with no match
XWILD1: SCF
        RET

;=======================================================================
; SETTINGS PERSISTENCE
;
; The settings live in a block inside this .COM, and saving them means
; writing that part of the file back over itself.  No second file to
; carry around, and a copy of EGT80.COM taken to another machine brings
; its settings with it - the way period software did it.
;
; Only the ONE 128-byte record holding the block is rewritten, using
; random-record write.  The record's contents are simply the memory it was
; loaded from, so the DMA address is pointed straight at the aligned image
; address and nothing has to be copied.
;
; CP/M never tells a program its own name, so the filename below is fixed:
; if EGT80.COM has been renamed, or is not on the current drive, the save
; is refused with a message rather than writing to something else.
;=======================================================================
SAVCFG: LD      DE,CFGFCB
        LD      C,BOPEN
        CALL    BDOS
        INC     A               ; 0FFH = not found
        JR      Z,SAVERR
        LD      A,CFGREC        ; which record the block sits in
        LD      (CFGFCB+33),A   ; random-record number, low byte
        XOR     A
        LD      (CFGFCB+34),A
        LD      (CFGFCB+35),A
        LD      DE,CFGADR       ; the aligned image address = the record
        LD      C,BSETDMA
        CALL    BDOS
        LD      DE,CFGFCB
        LD      C,BWRRAN
        CALL    BDOS
        OR      A
        JR      NZ,SAVERR
        LD      DE,CFGFCB
        LD      C,BCLOSE
        CALL    BDOS
        CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MSAVED
        CALL    PSTR
        RET
SAVERR: CALL    RSTDMA
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MSAVERR
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; RSTDMA - put the DMA address back where CP/M left it (0080H).  A
; program that moves it and does not restore it leaves the next BDOS file
; call writing into whatever it pointed at.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
RSTDMA: LD      DE,0080H
        LD      C,BSETDMA
        JP      BDOS

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CFGCHK - validate the settings block at startup.
;
; The signature guards against a patch written by something else, and the
; values are range-checked because a plausible-looking block with a
; nonsense port kind would send PORTST somewhere it should not go.  Any
; failure falls back to the defaults, so a damaged or hand-edited copy of
; EGT80.COM still starts in a usable state instead of misbehaving.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
CFGCHK: LD      HL,CFGSIG
        LD      DE,CFGREF
        LD      B,8
CFGCH1: LD      A,(DE)
        CP      (HL)
        JR      NZ,CFGBAD
        INC     HL
        INC     DE
        DEC     B
        JR      NZ,CFGCH1
        LD      A,(PKIND)       ; a kind we actually have a driver for
        CP      PKAUX+1
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(FLTMOD)      ; the flags are booleans
        CP      2
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(ECHOFL)
        CP      2
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(CRLFFL)
        CP      2
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        LD      A,(PABASE)      ; an ASCI base whose registers would run off
        CP      0F0H            ; the end of the I/O space is not usable
        JR      NC,CFGBAD
        RET
CFGBAD: LD      A,PKNONE        ; safe defaults: nothing selected, ANSI
        LD      (PKIND),A
        XOR     A
        LD      (PABASE),A
        LD      (PCHAN),A
        LD      (FLTMOD),A
        LD      (ECHOFL),A
        LD      (CRLFFL),A
        LD      A,19H           ; ^Y
        LD      (ESCCH),A
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MCFGBAD
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

;=======================================================================
; SCREENS
;=======================================================================

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCTTL - the opening screen.
;
; Plain text on purpose: the user has not yet told us whether this
; console understands ANSI, and a title screen that sprays escape
; sequences at a dumb terminal is a poor first impression.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCTTL:  CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MTITLE
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      HL,MRULE
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      HL,MVER
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCMENU - the main menu, with the current settings above it.
;
; The port and the filter mode are shown every time because they are the
; two settings that decide whether the program appears to work at all.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCMENU: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPORT
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHPORT
        LD      HL,MMODE
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHMODE
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MMENU
        CALL    PSTR
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHPORT - describe the selected port in the words the board's own
; documentation uses, followed by the actual addresses.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHPORT: LD      A,(PKIND)
        CP      PKSIO
        JR      Z,SHPSIO
        CP      PKACIA
        JR      Z,SHPACI
        CP      PKHBIO
        JR      Z,SHPHB
        CP      PKASCI
        JR      Z,SHPASC
        CP      PKAUX
        JR      Z,SHPAUX
        LD      HL,MPNONE
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      CRLF
SHPSIO: LD      HL,MPSIO
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      SHPADR
SHPACI: LD      HL,MPACIA
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      SHPADR
SHPHB:  LD      HL,MPHBIO
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        ADD     A,'0'           ; the unit number, not an address
        CALL    COUT
        JP      CRLF
SHPASC: LD      HL,MPASCI
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(PCHAN)
        ADD     A,'0'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      HL,MPBASE
        CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(PABASE)
        CALL    PHEX
        JP      CRLF
SHPAUX: LD      HL,MPAUX
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      CRLF

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHPADR - the shared "nn/nn" tail for the families that live at a pair
; of I/O addresses, printed in the form the board's documentation uses.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHPADR: LD      A,(PBASE)
        CALL    PHEX
        LD      A,'/'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,(PBASE)
        INC     A
        CALL    PHEX
        JP      CRLF

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHMODE - the filter mode, plus the two toggles that surprise people
; when they are set the other way.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHMODE: LD      A,(FLTMOD)
        OR      A
        LD      HL,MMANSI
        JR      Z,SHMOD1
        LD      HL,MMASC
SHMOD1: CALL    PSTR
        LD      A,(ECHOFL)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,SHMOD2
        LD      HL,MMECHO
        CALL    PSTR
SHMOD2: LD      A,(CRLFFL)
        OR      A
        JR      Z,SHMOD3
        LD      HL,MMCRLF
        CALL    PSTR
SHMOD3: JP      CRLF

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SHESC - name the escape key as a caret pair, e.g. ^Y.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SHESC:  LD      A,'^'
        CALL    COUT
        LD      A,(ESCCH)
        ADD     A,40H           ; control code to its printable letter
        JP      COUT


;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPORT - port selection, in two steps: the family, then which one.
;
; Families are listed with the machines they belong to rather than by
; chip name alone, because the user knows what they are sitting at.  The
; wrong choice produces silence rather than an error, so the help says so
; and the menu names real machines.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPORT: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPORTS
        CALL    PSTR
SCPOR1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      '1'
        JP      Z,SCPSIO
        CP      '2'
        JP      Z,SCPACI
        CP      '3'
        JP      Z,SCPHB
        CP      '4'
        JP      Z,SCPASC
        CP      '5'
        JP      Z,SCPAUX
        CP      'N'
        JR      Z,SCPONO
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCPOR1
SCPONO: XOR     A
        LD      (PKIND),A
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPSIO - Z80 SIO/2: four channels across two boards, or any address.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPSIO: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPSIOM
        CALL    PSTR
SCPSI1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      '1'
        JR      Z,SCPS80
        CP      '2'
        JR      Z,SCPS82
        CP      '3'
        JR      Z,SCPS84
        CP      '4'
        JR      Z,SCPS86
        CP      'B'
        JR      Z,SCPSBA
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCPSI1
SCPS80: LD      A,80H
        JR      SCPSSE
SCPS82: LD      A,82H
        JR      SCPSSE
SCPS84: LD      A,84H
        JR      SCPSSE
SCPS86: LD      A,86H
        JR      SCPSSE
SCPSBA: CALL    HEXASK          ; any address the board happens to use
        RET     C               ; cancelled: leave the port alone
SCPSSE: LD      (PBASE),A
        LD      A,PKSIO
        LD      (PKIND),A
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPACI - 6850 ACIA: the two Altair 88-2SIO ports, or any address.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPACI: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPACIM
        CALL    PSTR
SCPAC1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      '1'
        JR      Z,SCPA10
        CP      '2'
        JR      Z,SCPA12
        CP      'B'
        JR      Z,SCPABA
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCPAC1
SCPA10: LD      A,10H
        JR      SCPASE
SCPA12: LD      A,12H
        JR      SCPASE
SCPABA: CALL    HEXASK
        RET     C
SCPASE: LD      (PBASE),A
        LD      A,PKACIA
        LD      (PKIND),A
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPHB - RomWBW HBIOS: a unit number, not an address.
;
; Checked before it is accepted: HBIOS is reached by RST 8, and on a
; machine without it that instruction lands in unused memory and takes
; the program with it.  A RomWBW system puts a JP there, so the absence
; of one is a reliable "this is not a RomWBW machine" - far better to say
; so than to hang on the first keystroke.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPHB:  LD      A,(0008H)       ; the RST 8 vector
        CP      0C3H            ; a JP instruction?
        JR      Z,SCPHB1
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MNOHB
        CALL    PSTR
        RET
SCPHB1: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPHBM
        CALL    PSTR
SCPHB2: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        SUB     '0'
        CP      4               ; units 0-3
        JR      C,SCPHBS
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCPHB2
SCPHBS: LD      (PBASE),A
        LD      A,PKHBIO
        LD      (PKIND),A
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPASC - Z180 ASCI: a channel, and the internal I/O base if the system
; has moved it (RomWBW does on some boards).
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPASC: CALL    ISZ180          ; the ASCI is part of the CPU: is it there?
        JR      NC,SCPAS2
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MNOZ180
        CALL    PSTR
        RET
SCPAS2: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPASCM
        CALL    PSTR
SCPAS1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      '0'
        JR      Z,SCPAS0
        CP      '1'
        JR      Z,SCPASA
        CP      'B'
        JR      Z,SCPASB
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCPAS1
SCPAS0: XOR     A
        JR      SCPASS
SCPASA: LD      A,1
        JR      SCPASS
SCPASB: CALL    HEXASK          ; internal I/O base
        RET     C
        CP      0F0H            ; base+9 must not run off the top of the
        JR      NC,SCPASB       ; I/O space: ask again
        LD      (PABASE),A
        JR      SCPAS2          ; then still ask for the channel
SCPASS: LD      (PCHAN),A
        LD      A,PKASCI
        LD      (PKIND),A
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCPAUX - the CP/M AUX device: nothing to configure.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCPAUX: LD      A,PKAUX
        LD      (PKIND),A
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MAUXW
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      PDONE

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; PDONE - re-point the vectors at the new selection and show it.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
PDONE:  CALL    PORTST
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MPSET
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      SHPORT

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; HEXASK - read a two-digit hex byte.  Returns it in A, or carry set if
; the user gave up (RETURN or a bad digit), so a mistake never leaves a
; half-entered address behind.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
HEXASK: LD      HL,MHEXA
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    HEXDIG
        RET     C
        ADD     A,A
        ADD     A,A
        ADD     A,A
        ADD     A,A             ; high nibble
        LD      (HEXACC),A
        CALL    HEXDIG
        RET     C
        LD      HL,HEXACC
        OR      (HL)
        RET                     ; carry clear: A holds the byte

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; HEXDIG - read one hex digit, echo it, return its value in A.  Carry set
; on anything that is not a hex digit.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
HEXDIG: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      '0'
        JR      C,HEXBAD
        CP      '9'+1
        JR      C,HEXNUM
        CP      'A'
        JR      C,HEXBAD
        CP      'F'+1
        JR      NC,HEXBAD
        CALL    COUT
        SUB     'A'-10
        RET                     ; carry clear from SUB of a smaller value
HEXNUM: CALL    COUT
        SUB     '0'
        RET
HEXBAD: SCF
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCSETT - settings.  Each key toggles and the screen redraws, so the
; effect of a keypress is always visible.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCSETT: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MSETT
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHMODE
        LD      HL,MSETT2
        CALL    PSTR
SCSET1: CALL    CIN
        CALL    UPCASE
        CP      'A'
        JR      Z,TOGANS
        CP      'E'
        JR      Z,TOGECH
        CP      'L'
        JR      Z,TOGCRL
        CP      'K'
        JR      Z,CYCESC
        CP      'Q'
        RET     Z
        LD      A,BEL
        CALL    COUT
        JR      SCSET1
TOGANS: LD      HL,FLTMOD
        CALL    TOGGLE
        JR      SCSETT
TOGECH: LD      HL,ECHOFL
        CALL    TOGGLE
        JR      SCSETT
TOGCRL: LD      HL,CRLFFL
        CALL    TOGGLE
        JR      SCSETT
TOGGLE: LD      A,(HL)
        XOR     1
        LD      (HL),A
        RET

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CYCESC - step the escape key through the three characters period
; terminals use for it, so it can be moved off a key the remote system
; wants (^Y is WordStar's delete-line, for instance).
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
CYCESC: LD      A,(ESCCH)
        CP      19H             ; ^Y
        JR      Z,CYCE1
        CP      1DH             ; ^]
        JR      Z,CYCE2
        LD      A,19H           ; anything else: back to ^Y
        JR      CYCE3
CYCE1:  LD      A,1DH
        JR      CYCE3
CYCE2:  LD      A,1CH           ; ^\
CYCE3:  LD      (ESCCH),A
        CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MESCIS
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    SHESC
        CALL    CRLF
        JP      SCSETT

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCHELP - help.  The port choice is the one thing that decides whether
; this program works, so the help says so plainly and in the same terms
; the hardware documentation uses.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SCHELP: CALL    CRLF
        LD      HL,MHELP
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    TWAIT
        LD      HL,MHELP2
        CALL    PSTR
        CALL    TWAIT
        LD      HL,MHELP3
        CALL    PSTR
        JP      TWAIT

;=======================================================================
; TEXT
;
; All user-visible text lives here.  In a program this size the strings
; are the largest single part, so keeping them together makes that cost
; visible and the wording easy to review.
;=======================================================================
MTITLE: DB      'Ethernet Gateway Terminal',CR,LF,0
MRULE:  DB      '-------------------------',CR,LF,0
MVER:   DB      'EGT80 v0.4  CP/M 2.2 / CP/M 3  Z80',CR,LF,0

MPORT:  DB      'Port: ',0
MMODE:  DB      'Mode: ',0
MPNONE: DB      'none selected - press P',0
MPSIO:  DB      'Z80 SIO/2 at ',0
MPACIA: DB      '6850 ACIA at ',0
MPHBIO: DB      'RomWBW HBIOS unit ',0
MPASCI: DB      'Z180 ASCI channel ',0
MPBASE: DB      ', I/O base ',0
MPAUX:  DB      'CP/M AUX: device (BDOS 3/4)',0
MMANSI: DB      'ANSI (pass escape sequences)',0
MMASC:  DB      'ASCII (strip escape sequences)',0
MMECHO: DB      ' +echo',0
MMCRLF: DB      ' +CR/LF',0

MMENU:  DB      CR,LF
        DB      '  T  Terminal mode',CR,LF
        DB      '  P  Select serial port',CR,LF
        DB      '  S  Settings',CR,LF
        DB      '  U  Upload a file   (XMODEM, to the far end)',CR,LF
        DB      '  D  Download a file (XMODEM, from the far end)',CR,LF
        DB      '  H  Help',CR,LF
        DB      '  V  Save settings into EGT80.COM',CR,LF
        DB      '  X  Exit to CP/M',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0

MPORTS: DB      'Select serial port',CR,LF
        DB      '------------------',CR,LF
        DB      '  1  Z80 SIO/2     - RC2014, RomWBW, many Z80 boards',CR,LF
        DB      '  2  6850 ACIA     - Altair 88-2SIO, RC2014 ACIA',CR,LF
        DB      '  3  RomWBW HBIOS  - any RomWBW machine (RST 8)',CR,LF
        DB      '  4  Z180 ASCI     - SC126 and other Z180 boards',CR,LF
        DB      '  5  CP/M AUX:     - portable fallback, see Help',CR,LF
        DB      '  N  None',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Leave unchanged',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MPSIOM: DB      'Z80 SIO/2 channel',CR,LF
        DB      '  1  board 1 channel A - 80/81',CR,LF
        DB      '  2  board 1 channel B - 82/83  (usual AUX: port)',CR,LF
        DB      '  3  board 2 channel A - 84/85',CR,LF
        DB      '  4  board 2 channel B - 86/87',CR,LF
        DB      '  B  some other address',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Back',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MPACIM: DB      '6850 ACIA port',CR,LF
        DB      '  1  88-2SIO port 1 - 10/11',CR,LF
        DB      '  2  88-2SIO port 2 - 12/13',CR,LF
        DB      '  B  some other address',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Back',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MPHBM:  DB      'RomWBW HBIOS serial unit (0-3), Q to go back: ',0
MNOHB:  DB      'This machine has no RST 8 vector, so it is not',CR,LF
        DB      'running RomWBW - HBIOS is not available here.',CR,LF
        DB      'Pick the chip your board uses instead.',CR,LF,0
MNOZ180: DB     'This processor is not a Z180, so it has no ASCI',CR,LF
        DB      'ports.  Pick the serial chip your board uses.',CR,LF,0
MPASCM: DB      'Z180 ASCI channel',CR,LF
        DB      '  0  channel 0',CR,LF
        DB      '  1  channel 1',CR,LF
        DB      '  B  set the internal I/O base first',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Back',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MAUXW:  DB      'CP/M AUX: selected.  Note that CP/M 2.2 has no way',CR,LF
        DB      'to ask whether a byte is waiting, so this family',CR,LF
        DB      'reads ahead and cannot receive a literal ^Z.  Use a',CR,LF
        DB      'chip family instead when one fits the machine.',CR,LF,0
MHEXA:  DB      CR,LF,'Address in hex (two digits): ',0
MPSET:  DB      'Port is now: ',0

MSETT:  DB      'Settings',CR,LF
        DB      '--------',CR,LF
        DB      'Now: ',0
MSETT2: DB      CR,LF
        DB      '  A  ANSI / ASCII output',CR,LF
        DB      '  E  Local echo on / off',CR,LF
        DB      '  L  Send LF after a typed CR',CR,LF
        DB      '  K  Change the menu key',CR,LF
        DB      '  Q  Back',CR,LF
        DB      CR,LF,'Choice: ',0
MESCIS: DB      'Menu key is now ',0

MTERM:  DB      'Terminal mode.  Press ',0
MTERM2: DB      ' for the menu.',CR,LF,CR,LF,0
MTEXIT: DB      'Leaving terminal mode.',CR,LF,0
MESCMN: DB      'E)xit H)elp S)et U)pload D)ownload (twice=send): ',0
MNOPRT: DB      CR,LF,'No serial port is selected yet.',CR,LF
        DB      'Press P at the menu and pick the one your',CR,LF
        DB      'machine uses.',CR,LF,0

MDEAD:  DB      'Nothing was sent: the port did not accept the',CR,LF
        DB      'character.  Selected port is ',0
MDEAD2: DB      'Either no serial chip answers at that address,',CR,LF
        DB      'or it is held off by flow control.  Press P at',CR,LF
        DB      'the menu to pick a different port.',CR,LF,0

MXSFIL: DB      CR,LF,'Send which file? ',0
MXRFIL: DB      CR,LF,'Receive as which file? ',0
MXNONAM: DB     'No filename given - nothing done.',CR,LF,0
MXNOFIL: DB     CR,LF,'No such file on that drive.',CR,LF,0
MXEXIST: DB     CR,LF,'That file exists.  Replace it (Y/N)?  The old',CR,LF
        DB      'one is deleted before the transfer starts. ',0
MXKEEP: DB      'Kept the existing file - nothing done.',CR,LF,0
MXNOMK: DB      CR,LF,'Could not create the file (directory full?).',CR,LF,0
MXWAIT: DB      CR,LF,'Waiting for the other end to start receiving...',CR,LF,0
MXGO:   DB      CR,LF,'Transferring ( . = block, ? = retry )',CR,LF,0
MXSENT: DB      'Sent.',CR,LF,0
MXRECVD: DB     'Received.',CR,LF,0
MXFAIL: DB      'Transfer failed - the other end stopped answering.',CR,LF,0
MXCAN:  DB      'The other end cancelled the transfer.',CR,LF,0
MXSCRAP: DB     'The incomplete file has been deleted.',CR,LF,0
MXUSER: DB      'Stopped at this end.',CR,LF,0
MXDISK: DB      'Disk full or write failed - transfer stopped.',CR,LF,0

MSAVED: DB      'Settings saved into EGT80.COM.',CR,LF,0
MSAVERR: DB     'Could not save: EGT80.COM must be on the current',CR,LF
        DB      'drive under its own name.  Settings still apply',CR,LF
        DB      'for this run.',CR,LF,0
MCFGBAD: DB     'Saved settings were not valid - defaults restored.',CR,LF,0
CFGREF: DB      'EGT80CFG'      ; what CFGSIG must contain

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
; CFGFCB - a fixed FCB for our own file.  CP/M never tells a program its
; own name, so this is the one place EGT80.COM is hard-coded; a renamed or
; off-drive copy makes the save fail with a message instead of writing to
; the wrong file.  Drive byte 0 = whichever drive is current.
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------
CFGFCB: DB      0
        DB      'EGT80   '
        DB      'COM'
        DB      0,0,0,0
        DB      0,0,0,0,0,0,0,0
        DB      0,0,0,0,0,0,0,0
        DB      0               ; cr
        DB      0,0,0           ; r0/r1/r2 - the random record number

MKEY:   DB      CR,LF,'Press any key. ',0
MBYE:   DB      'EGT80 done.',CR,LF,0

MHELP:  DB      'Help - the serial port',CR,LF
        DB      '----------------------',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'Choosing the port is the one setting that decides',CR,LF
        DB      'whether EGT80 works.  Get it wrong and nothing is',CR,LF
        DB      'sent and nothing arrives - no error, just silence.',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'Z80 SIO/2   RC2014 and most Z80 boards.  On RC2014',CR,LF
        DB      '            or RomWBW, 82/83 is the usual channel.',CR,LF
        DB      '6850 ACIA   Altair 88-2SIO (10/11, 12/13) and the',CR,LF
        DB      '            RC2014 ACIA boards.',CR,LF
        DB      'RomWBW      Asks the firmware to move the byte, so',CR,LF
        DB      'HBIOS       it works on any RomWBW machine whatever',CR,LF
        DB      '            the hardware.  Needs RomWBW.',CR,LF
        DB      'Z180 ASCI   The serial ports inside a Z180, as on',CR,LF
        DB      '            an SC126.',CR,LF
        DB      'CP/M AUX:   Portable fallback - read help page 3',CR,LF
        DB      '            before choosing it.',CR,LF,0
MHELP2: DB      'Help - using the terminal',CR,LF
        DB      '-------------------------',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'Terminal mode passes what you type to the port and',CR,LF
        DB      'what arrives to your screen.  The menu key',CR,LF
        DB      'interrupts it; pressing that key twice sends it to',CR,LF
        DB      'the remote end instead, and Settings can move it if',CR,LF
        DB      'the remote system wants that key for itself.',CR,LF
        DB      'ANSI mode passes escape sequences through to your',CR,LF
        DB      'terminal.  ASCII mode strips them, drops the high',CR,LF
        DB      'bit and discards control codes that are not CR, LF,',CR,LF
        DB      'TAB, BS or BEL - which suits a printing terminal or',CR,LF
        DB      'a console with no cursor addressing: a remote menu',CR,LF
        DB      'drawn with graphics still reads as text.',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'Settings are saved into EGT80.COM itself, so a copy',CR,LF
        DB      'of the program carries them to another disk.  The',CR,LF
        DB      'file has to be on the current drive under its own',CR,LF
        DB      'name for that to work.',CR,LF,0
MHELP3: DB      'Help - two things to know',CR,LF
        DB      '-------------------------',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'CP/M AUX: has no way to ask whether a byte is',CR,LF
        DB      'waiting - the operating system offers no such call.',CR,LF
        DB      'EGT80 therefore reads one byte ahead, which means',CR,LF
        DB      'this family cannot receive a literal ^Z, and on real',CR,LF
        DB      'CP/M 2.2 hardware the read waits instead of',CR,LF
        DB      'returning.  Against the Ethernet Gateway it works',CR,LF
        DB      'well; on real hardware prefer the family that',CR,LF
        DB      'matches your serial chip.',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'The Z180 ASCI registers are Z180 internal I/O.  If',CR,LF
        DB      'your system has moved the internal I/O base away',CR,LF
        DB      'from 00, set that base first with B on the ASCI',CR,LF
        DB      'menu, then pick the channel.',CR,LF,CR,LF
        DB      'Transfers are plain XMODEM, 128-byte blocks, CRC',CR,LF
        DB      'with the checksum fallback an older peer may want.',CR,LF
        DB      'Start the far end first, then press U or D - from',CR,LF
        DB      'the menu or from the terminal-mode menu key, which',CR,LF
        DB      'does not hang up.  A dot is a block, a ? a retry.',CR,LF,0

;=======================================================================
; VECTORS AND VARIABLES
;
; The vectors are JP instructions rewritten at run time; the 0 operands
; below are placeholders that CONSET and PORTST fill in before anything
; can call them.
;=======================================================================
VCST:   JP      0               ; console status
VCIN:   JP      0               ; console input
VCOUT:  JP      0               ; console output
VPST:   JP      0               ; port receive status
VPIN:   JP      0               ; port input
VPOST:  JP      0               ; port transmit status
VPOUT:  JP      0               ; port output

CPMVER: DB      0               ; BDOS version, from CONSET
FSTATE: DB      0               ; inbound escape-sequence state
ESTATE: DB      0               ; local-echo escape-sequence state
SENDCH: DB      0               ; byte in flight in PSEND
PTRIES: DB      0               ; PSEND give-up counter
PDEAD:  DB      0               ; set when the transmitter never freed
TYPECH: DB      0               ; byte in flight in TSEND
HEXACC: DB      0               ; high nibble held while HEXASK reads the low
XBLK:   DB      0               ; block number being sent / expected
XGOTBL: DB      0               ; block number as it arrived
XCRCM:  DB      0               ; non-zero = CRC mode, zero = checksum
XCRC:   DW      0               ; CRC-16 accumulator
XSUM:   DB      0               ; checksum accumulator
XCNT:   DB      0               ; retries left
XNCNT:  DB      0               ; junk bytes still tolerated
XRXACT: DB      0               ; non-zero while a download's file exists
XTMO:   DB      0               ; XGETB timeout, in 65536-poll passes
XTMOC:  DB      0               ; ...and its working copy
XLINE:  DS      24              ; console line buffer for a filename
XFCB:   DS      36              ; FCB for the file being transferred
XBUF:   DS      128             ; the block, which is also the CP/M record
AUXHLD: DB      0               ; byte read ahead by the AUX status call
AUXFLG: DB      0               ; non-zero while AUXHLD holds a byte

; Stack.  128 bytes = 64 levels, which is more than this program's own
; call depth needs (about a dozen levels at the deepest, inside a
; transfer) - the margin is for the system underneath: a real CP/M BIOS
; may use the CALLER's stack, and the BDOS file calls a transfer makes
; are exactly where that would bite.  It matters here because the stack
; grows down towards XBUF, so an overflow would quietly corrupt the block
; being transferred rather than crashing where it happened.
        DS      128
STKTOP  EQU     $

        END     START
